		The browser trusts the website
		So it also trusts the malicious script, thinking it's legitimate
	5. The server is not compromised, the user is
		
** Test 9 **
Volumetric DDoS 
	Overwhelms network bandwidth with high traffic volume
Amplified DDoS
	Uses a vulnerable third party server to amplify traffic sent to the target
		Small request -> Huge response sent to the victim
		Makes the attack more efficient: fewer attacker resources required
Reflected DDoS
	Similar to amplified, but emphasizes reflecting traffic off another system
		Attack spoofs victim IP in requests
		Servers respond to the victim instead of attacker
		Often combined with amplification for even bigger effect
Application DDoS
	Targets the application layer instead of network bandwidth
		Focused on specific functions: HTTP request, login forms
		Harder to detect because traffic can look legitimate
Credential stuffing
	An attack where an attacker takes username/password pairs that were stolen from one website and automatically
	tests them on many other websites to find accounts that reuse the same credentials
	Relies on password reuse, not brute force
DNS Spoofing
	Also called DNS Poisoning, is an attack where an attacker corrupts DNS information so a victim's DNS query
	returns a malicious IP address instead of a legitimate one
	As a result:
		Redirected to fake websites, malware sites, phishing portals, attacker-controlled servers
URL Hijacking (Typosquatting)
Domain Hijacking
	When an attacker takes unauthorized control of a domain name, allowing them to change DNS records
	transfer ownership, or redirect traffic
ARP Poisoning (ARP Spoofing)
	An attack where an attacker sends forged ARP messages on a local network to associate their MAC address with the 
	IP address of another device (usually the gateway/router)
	How it works:
	1.	ARP maps IP -> MAC on the local network (layer 2)
		ARP has no authentication, so devices trust whatever ARP reply they receive
	2.	Enables MITM attacks
		By poisoning the ARP cache, the attacker can:
			Intercept traffic, Modify packets, redirect traffic, drop packets (DoS)
	3.	Works only on local networks so the attacker must be on the same subnet or VLAN
Sybil Attack
	An attack where one adversary creates many fake identities (nodes, accounts, machines, or personas)
	to gain disproportionate influence over a system
Jump Server (jump box, or jump host)
	Hardened, controlled entry point used to access devices on a secure or isolated network.
War Driving
	A wireless reconnaissance technique where an attacker drives around with a laptop or mobile devices
	searching for wifi networks, often to find unsecured or poorly secured ones
Pass the Hash - PtH
	An authentication attack where an attacker uses a stolen password hash to authenticate to systems without ever
	needing to know the actual password
LDAP Injection Attack
	A type of injection attack where an attacker inserts malicious LDAP statements into user supplied fields to 
	manipulate or bypass authentication and directory queries
CSRF/XSRF Attack
	Cross Site Request Forgery is an attack where a malicious website tricks a users browser into performing
	actions on a trusted website where the user is already authenticated
XML Injection Attack
	An attack where an attacker inserts malicious XML content or modifies XML data structures to manipulate
	how an application processes XML
ARP Poisoning vs DNS Poisoning
	ARP Poisoning
		When an attacker sends fake ARP messages on a LAN to associate their MAC address with another devices IP addr
		Goal: to redirect LAN traffic through the attacker
		Enabling: MITM, Session Hijacking, Traffic Sniffing, DoS
		Only works inside the same broadcast domain
	DNS Cache Poisoning
		When an attacker inserts false DNS records into a DNS resolvers cache
		Goal: To redirect victims to malicious websites
		Characteristics: Works across the internet, not just LAN, Targets DNS servers, resolvers, or local machine 
				DNS caches

