** Test 14 **
RAID 0 - Striping
	Minimum Disks: 2
	Redundancy: None
	Performance: Fastest read/write
	Capacity: 100% Usable
	How it Works:
		Data is split evenly across disks
		If one drive dies -> All data is lost
	Best For:
		High speed temporary data, Gaming PCs, Caches, Non critial performance workloads
RAID 1 - Mirroring
	Minimum Disks: 2
	Redundancy: Full
	Performance: Reads - faster, Writes - slightly slower
	Capacity: 50%
	How it Works:
		Each disk contains an exact copy of the data
		If 1 disk fails -> system keeps running
	Best For: 
		OS drives, Small business servers, Systems needing high availability with small data sets
RAID 5 - Striping + Single Parity
	Minimum Disks: 3
	Redundancy: Can survive 1 disk failure
	Performance: Reads - fast, Writes - slower (parity calc)
	Capacity: (N-1)
	How it Works:
		Data is striped, and parity (recovery info) is stored across disks
		If 1 disk dies -> Data is rebuilt from parity
	Best for:
		File servers, Backup servers, Environments needing balance of speed + capacity + redundancy
RAID 6 - Striping + Double Parity
	Minimum Disks: 4
	Redundancy: Can survive 2 disk failures
	Performance: Reads - fast, Writes - slowest (double parity calc)
	Capacity: (N-2)
	How it Works:
		Same as RAID 5 but with 2 sets of parity
		Much safer with large disks (3TB+) where rebuilds take days
	Best for:
		Large storage arrays, Mission critical data, Big data environments, Systems where rebuild is long and risky
RAID 10 - Raid 1 + Raid 0 (Mirrored Stripes)
	Minimum Disks: 4
	Redundancy: Can survive multiple disk failures as long as they aren't both in the same mirrored pair
	Performance: Very fast
	Capcity: 50%
	How it Works:
		1. Data is mirrored
		2. Then mirrored sets are striped
		Gives the speed of RAID 0 + safety of RAID 1
	Best for:
		Databases
		Virtualization hosts
		High performance applications
		Systems needing fast writes + high availability
